      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait i think i see what happened. i checked the execution role and it definitely has the s3:PutObject permission too. i also checked the bucket name and the arn looks right. \n\nis it possible i need more than just PutObject? like, do i need permissions on the bucket itself and not just the objects inside? i might have missed something about the bucket level vs the object level. can u clarify if that could be the issue?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, that is a very likely cause! When using the CloudWatch logs API on S3, the Lambda function effectively performs two specific actions: `s3:PutObject` to write the log file, and crucially, `s3:GetBucketLocation` (sometimes handled by the underlying `srv:GetBucketLog` logic) to ensure the bucket is configured for multipart uploads or specific logging settings. If your action list only includes `PutObject`, it will fail if the underlying service principals attempt the bucket-level check first. You should update your policy to include `s3:*` or explicitly add `s3:ListBucket` to cover these necessary operations on the bucket itself.",
      "source": "target",
